input.prompt:
In this repo (a JS project with an ISBN helper library), there's a bug: ISBN-10s that end in the check character 'X' (e.g. "0-8044-2957-X") are always reported as invalid by `isValidIsbn10`.

Root cause, already located: in `src/isbn.mjs`, function `isValidIsbn10` (around line 7-13):

```js
export function isValidIsbn10(raw) {
  const s = normalise(raw);
  if (!/^\d{9}[\dX]$/.test(s)) return false;
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) sum += (10 - i) * parseInt(s[i], 10);
  return sum % 11 === 0;
}
```

The loop uses `parseInt(s[i], 10)` for every character including the last one. When the last character is `'X'` (representing check value 10 in ISBN-10 checksums), `parseInt('X', 10)` returns `NaN`, so `sum` becomes `NaN` and `sum % 11 === 0` is always false. That's why valid ISBN-10s ending in X are rejected.

Fix: in the loop, when `s[i] === 'X'`, use the value 10 instead of `parseInt(s[i], 10)`. Keep the rest of the function logic and style consistent with the file (see also `toIsbn13`, `parseIsbn` in the same file, and `normalise` which already uppercases input so only uppercase 'X' needs to be handled).

After fixing:
1. Check `test/isbn.test.mjs` for existing test structure/conventions and add a test case (if not already covered) verifying that "0-8044-2957-X" (and/or its normalised form "080442957X") is now valid, and that the checksum math is still correct for a normal ISBN-10 without X.
2. Run the project's test suite (check package.json for the test script, likely `npm test`) and confirm everything passes.
3. Also double check `parseIsbn` and `toIsbn13` in the same file still work correctly for an ISBN-10 ending in X (toIsbn13 should work since it calls isValidIsbn10 and then only uses s.slice(0,9), so the X handling there was less critical, but verify).

Report back concisely what you changed and the test results.

